{"name":"Honey Crisp","hex":"#e9c160","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"honey","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sweet, viscous, gold-colored fluid produced from plant nectar by bees, and often consumed by humans.","A variety of this substance."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Involving or resembling honey.","Of a pale yellow to brownish-yellow color, like most types of honey."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/honey"},{"term":"crisp","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Senses relating to curliness.\n Of hair: curling, especially in tight, stiff curls or ringlets; also , of a person: having hair curled in this manner.","Of hair: curling, especially in tight, stiff curls or ringlets; also , of a person: having hair curled in this manner."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Senses relating to something brittle.\n In full potato crisp: a thin slice of potato which has been deep-fried until it is brittle and crispy, and eaten when cool; they are typically packaged and sold as a snack.","In full potato crisp: a thin slice of potato which has been deep-fried until it is brittle and crispy, and eaten when cool; they are typically packaged and sold as a snack."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crisp"}],"entries":[{"title":"Honeycrisp","blocks":[{"text":"Honeycrisp is an apple cultivar developed at the University of Minnesota's Horticultural Research Center in Chaska, Minnesota. Designated as MN1711 in 1974, patented in 1988, and released in 1991, the Honeycrisp, once slated to be discarded, has rapidly become a prized commercial agricultural product, as its sweetness, firmness, and tartness make it an ideal apple for eating raw. It may also be used for baking and making apple sauce. The advent of the Honeycrisp marks a turning point in the history of the apple industry. As of 2006, the Honeycrisp apple cultivar is now identified as the official state fruit of Minnesota. A large-sized Honeycrisp contains about 116 kilocalories (490 kJ)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Honeycrisp-Apple.jpg/330px-Honeycrisp-Apple.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honeycrisp","wikidata":"Q3140024"},{"title":"Honey","blocks":[{"text":"Honey is a sweet and viscous substance made by several species of bees, the best-known of which are honey bees. Honey is made and stored to nourish bee colonies. Bees produce honey by gathering and then refining the sugary secretions of plants or the secretions of other insects, like the honeydew of aphids. This refinement takes place both within individual bees, through regurgitation and enzymatic activity, and during storage in the hive, through water evaporation that concentrates the honey's sugars until it is thick and viscous."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Runny_hunny.jpg/330px-Runny_hunny.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honey","wikidata":"Q10987"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Honeycrisp_apple_orchard.jpg/500px-Honeycrisp_apple_orchard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Honeycrisp_apple_orchard.jpg/1280px-Honeycrisp_apple_orchard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Honeycrisp_apple_orchard.jpg/500px-Honeycrisp_apple_orchard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Honeycrisp apple orchard in Annapolis Valley, Nova Scotia, Canada (2009)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHoneycrisp_apple_orchard.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/93/US_Apple_Production_5-yr_Average_%282021-25%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/93/US_Apple_Production_5-yr_Average_%282021-25%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"The Honeycrisp continues to be one of the top apple varieties in the United States during the first half of the 2020s.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUS_Apple_Production_5-yr_Average_(2021-25).png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Miels_d%27Alsace_agriculture_biologique.jpg/500px-Miels_d%27Alsace_agriculture_biologique.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Miels_d%27Alsace_agriculture_biologique.jpg/1280px-Miels_d%27Alsace_agriculture_biologique.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Miels_d%27Alsace_agriculture_biologique.jpg/500px-Miels_d%27Alsace_agriculture_biologique.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"French honey from different floral sources, with visible differences in color and texture","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMiels_d'Alsace_agriculture_biologique.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Bee_on_-calyx_935.jpg/500px-Bee_on_-calyx_935.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Bee_on_-calyx_935.jpg/500px-Bee_on_-calyx_935.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A honey bee with its proboscis extended into a calyx of goldenrod","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABee_on_-calyx_935.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Honey_comb.jpg/500px-Honey_comb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Honey_comb.jpg/500px-Honey_comb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Honeycomb displaying hexagonal prismatic wax cells in which honey bees store honey","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHoney_comb.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Sealed_Honey_in_frame.JPG/500px-Sealed_Honey_in_frame.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Sealed_Honey_in_frame.JPG/500px-Sealed_Honey_in_frame.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sealed frame of honey","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASealed_Honey_in_frame.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Extractor_Beekeeping.jpg/500px-Extractor_Beekeeping.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Extractor_Beekeeping.jpg/1280px-Extractor_Beekeeping.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Extractor_Beekeeping.jpg/500px-Extractor_Beekeeping.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Extraction from a honeycomb","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AExtractor_Beekeeping.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Filtering_of_honey.jpg/500px-Filtering_of_honey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Filtering_of_honey.jpg/1280px-Filtering_of_honey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wik